In JavaScript, a **prototype** is an object that allows **other objects to inherit properties and methods**.  
Every JavaScript object has an internal property called **`[[Prototype]]`**, which links it to another object.

This mechanism forms the **prototype chain**, enabling **inheritance in JavaScript**.

---

**1. Basic Example**

```javascript
function Person(name){
  this.name = name;
}

Person.prototype.greet = function(){
  console.log("Hello " + this.name);
};

const p1 = new Person("Prem");

p1.greet(); // Hello Prem
```

Here:

```
p1 → Person.prototype → Object.prototype → null
```

`p1` inherits the `greet()` method from `Person.prototype`.

---

**2. Why Prototypes Are Used**

If methods are inside the constructor, each object gets its own copy.

```javascript
function User(name){
  this.name = name;

  this.sayHi = function(){
    console.log("Hi");
  };
}
```

Better approach using prototype:

```javascript
function User(name){
  this.name = name;
}

User.prototype.sayHi = function(){
  console.log("Hi");
};
```

Now **all objects share one method**, saving memory.

---

**3. Prototype Chain**

```javascript
const arr = [1,2,3];

console.log(arr.__proto__ === Array.prototype); // true
```

Chain structure:

```
arr
 ↓
Array.prototype
 ↓
Object.prototype
 ↓
null
```

---

**Summary**

- Every JavaScript object has a **prototype**  
- Prototypes enable **inheritance and method sharing**  
- Objects access properties through the **prototype chain**.

