**Memoization** is an optimization technique where the **results of expensive function calls are cached**, so that when the same inputs occur again, the stored result is returned instead of recalculating.

This improves performance by **avoiding repeated computations**.

---

**Basic Example**

```javascript
function memoizedAdd() {

  const cache = {};

  return function(num){

    if(cache[num]){
      return cache[num];
    }

    console.log("Calculating...");

    const result = num + 10;

    cache[num] = result;

    return result;
  }
}

const add = memoizedAdd();

console.log(add(5)); 
console.log(add(5));
```

Output

```
Calculating...
15
15
```

Second call uses the **cached result**.

---

**Example with Fibonacci**

```javascript
function fib(n, cache = {}){

  if(n <= 1) return n;

  if(cache[n]) return cache[n];

  cache[n] = fib(n-1, cache) + fib(n-2, cache);

  return cache[n];
}

console.log(fib(10));
```

This reduces time complexity drastically.

---

**Memoization in React**

```javascript
const result = useMemo(()=>{
  return expensiveCalculation(data);
},[data]);
```

---

**Summary**

```
Memoization
 → caches function results
 → avoids repeated calculations
 → improves performance
```

Common use cases:

- recursive algorithms  
- expensive computations  
- React performance optimization.

**WeakMap** and **WeakSet** are special collections in JavaScript that store **weak references to objects**, allowing them to be **garbage collected automatically when no other references exist**.

They help prevent **memory leaks**.

---

**1. WeakMap**

A **WeakMap** stores **key–value pairs**, where **keys must be objects**.

Features:

- Keys must be objects  
- Keys are weakly referenced  
- Not iterable  

Example:

```javascript id="p9zzrb"
const weakMap = new WeakMap();

let user = { name: "Prem" };

weakMap.set(user, "Developer");

console.log(weakMap.get(user));
```

If the object reference is removed:

```javascript id="7bgdpy"
user = null;
```

The entry in `WeakMap` becomes **eligible for garbage collection**.

---

**2. WeakSet**

A **WeakSet** stores **unique objects only**.

Features:

- Only objects allowed  
- Weak references  
- Not iterable  

Example:

```javascript id="ju6ka0"
const weakSet = new WeakSet();

let obj = { id: 1 };

weakSet.add(obj);

console.log(weakSet.has(obj));
```

If the object is removed:

```javascript id="gu8g6e"
obj = null;
```

The object can be **garbage collected automatically**.

---

**3. WeakMap vs WeakSet**

```text id="khblwq"
Feature     WeakMap           WeakSet
-------------------------------------------
Data        key-value pairs   objects only
Keys        objects only      objects only
Iterable    No                No
Use case    private data      object tracking
```

---

**Common Use Case**

```javascript id="0cxx2r"
const privateData = new WeakMap();

class User {

  constructor(name){
    privateData.set(this, {name});
  }

  getName(){
    return privateData.get(this).name;
  }

}
```

