## Singleton Pattern (Java)

The **Singleton pattern** is a design pattern that ensures **only one instance of a class exists in the JVM and provides a global access point to it**.

---

## Basic Singleton Implementation

```java id="vcm7p0"
public class Singleton {

    private static Singleton instance;

    private Singleton(){}

    public static Singleton getInstance(){

        if(instance == null){
            instance = new Singleton();
        }

        return instance;
    }

}
```

Usage:

```java id="xbkmc1"
public class Demo {

    public static void main(String[] args){

        Singleton s1 = Singleton.getInstance();
        Singleton s2 = Singleton.getInstance();

        System.out.println(s1 == s2);

    }

}
```

Output

``` id="cufcuv"
true
```

Only **one object created**.

---

## Problems with Basic Singleton

Not thread-safe.

Example issue:

``` id="vvx4m8"
Two threads call getInstance()
Both see instance == null
Two objects created
```

---

## Thread-Safe Singleton

```java id="vqqfui"
public class Singleton {

    private static Singleton instance;

    private Singleton(){}

    public static synchronized Singleton getInstance(){

        if(instance == null){
            instance = new Singleton();
        }

        return instance;
    }

}
```

But synchronization is **slow**.

---

## Double-Checked Locking (Best Practice)

```java id="i6ty8u"
public class Singleton {

    private static volatile Singleton instance;

    private Singleton(){}

    public static Singleton getInstance(){

        if(instance == null){

            synchronized(Singleton.class){

                if(instance == null){
                    instance = new Singleton();
                }

            }

        }

        return instance;
    }

}
```

---

## Eager Initialization Singleton

```java id="5sbnoy"
public class Singleton {

    private static final Singleton instance = new Singleton();

    private Singleton(){}

    public static Singleton getInstance(){
        return instance;
    }

}
```

Object created **when class loads**.

---

## Best Singleton (Using Enum)

Recommended by **Effective Java**.

```java id="ie71sw"
public enum Singleton {

    INSTANCE;

    public void show(){
        System.out.println("Singleton instance");
    }

}
```

Usage:

```java id="srq2iv"
Singleton.INSTANCE.show();
```

---

## Where Singleton Is Used

``` id="ohzpej"
Logger
Configuration manager
Database connection pool
Cache manager
Spring beans (default singleton scope)
```

---

## Key Points

``` id="48d4ao"
Private constructor
Static instance
Global access method
Ensures single object
```

---

## One-line Interview Answer

**The Singleton pattern ensures that a class has only one instance and provides a global access point to that instance.**

`equals()` and `hashCode()` are used together when objects are stored in **hash-based collections** like `HashMap`, `HashSet`, etc.

---

## 1. `equals()` Method

Used to compare **logical equality (object content)**.

Example:

```java
class User {

    int id;
    String name;

    User(int id, String name){
        this.id = id;
        this.name = name;
    }

    @Override
    public boolean equals(Object obj){

        if(this == obj) return true;

        if(obj == null || getClass() != obj.getClass())
            return false;

        User user = (User) obj;

        return id == user.id &&
               name.equals(user.name);
    }
}
```

Usage:

```java
User u1 = new User(1,"Prem");
User u2 = new User(1,"Prem");

System.out.println(u1.equals(u2)); // true
```

---

## 2. `hashCode()` Method

Returns an **integer hash value** used for **hash table bucket calculation**.

Example:

```java
@Override
public int hashCode() {
    return Objects.hash(id, name);
}
```

Usage:

```java
User u1 = new User(1,"Prem");
User u2 = new User(1,"Prem");

System.out.println(u1.hashCode());
System.out.println(u2.hashCode());
```

Both produce **same hash code**.

---

## 3. Contract Between `equals()` and `hashCode()`

Java rule:

```text
If two objects are equal according to equals(),
they MUST have the same hashCode().
```

Example:

```java
User u1 = new User(1,"Prem");
User u2 = new User(1,"Prem");

System.out.println(u1.equals(u2));   // true
System.out.println(u1.hashCode() == u2.hashCode()); // true
```

---

## 4. Why This Matters (HashMap Example)

```java
Map<User,String> map = new HashMap<>();

map.put(new User(1,"Prem"), "Developer");

String role = map.get(new User(1,"Prem"));

System.out.println(role);
```

Without proper `equals()` and `hashCode()` → result:

```text
null
```

Because HashMap uses:

```
hashCode() → bucket selection
equals()   → key comparison
```

---

## 5. Quick Comparison

| Feature | equals() | hashCode() |
|---|---|---|
| Purpose | Compare object content | Generate hash value |
| Return type | boolean | int |
| Used by | Logical equality | Hash-based collections |
| Must override together | Yes | Yes |

---

## One-line Interview Answer

**`equals()` checks logical equality between objects, while `hashCode()` returns a hash value used by hash-based collections like HashMap to locate the object's bucket efficiently.**

