Yes, **normally the `finally` block always executes**, but there are **rare cases where it can be skipped**.

---

## Normal Behavior

`finally` runs **whether an exception occurs or not**.

```java
public class Demo {

    public static void main(String[] args) {

        try {

            int x = 10 / 0;

        } catch (Exception e) {

            System.out.println("Catch block");

        } finally {

            System.out.println("Finally block");

        }

    }
}
```

Output

```
Catch block
Finally block
```

---

## Cases When `finally` May Be Skipped

### 1. `System.exit()`

If JVM exits explicitly.

```java
public class Demo {

    public static void main(String[] args) {

        try {

            System.out.println("Try block");
            System.exit(0);

        } finally {

            System.out.println("Finally block");

        }

    }
}
```

Output

```
Try block
```

`finally` does **not execute**.

---

### 2. JVM Crash

Example:

```
OutOfMemoryError
JVM crash
Power failure
```

Then `finally` may not execute.

---

### 3. Infinite Loop Before Finally

```java
try {
    while(true){}
} finally {
    System.out.println("Finally");
}
```

`finally` never reached.

---

## Important Point

Even if `return` exists in `try`, `finally` still executes.

Example:

```java
public class Demo {

    public static void main(String[] args) {

        System.out.println(test());

    }

    static int test() {

        try {
            return 10;
        } finally {
            System.out.println("Finally executed");
        }

    }
}
```

Output

```
Finally executed
10
```

---

## One-line Interview Answer

**The `finally` block normally always executes, but it can be skipped if the JVM terminates (e.g., `System.exit()`), crashes, or the program never reaches the finally block.**

In Java, **everything is passed by value**.  
This means the **method receives a copy of the variable**, not the original variable itself.

---

## 1. Pass-by-value with Primitive Types

When primitives are passed, **a copy of the value is passed**.

Example:

```java
public class PassByValueDemo {

    public static void main(String[] args) {

        int x = 10;

        changeValue(x);

        System.out.println(x); // 10

    }

    static void changeValue(int num) {
        num = 50;
    }
}
```

Flow:

```text
x = 10
copy of x → num
num = 50

original x remains 10
```

Output

```
10
```

---

## 2. Pass-by-value with Objects

For objects, Java passes **a copy of the reference**.

Example:

```java
class User {
    String name;
}

public class PassByValueObject {

    public static void main(String[] args) {

        User u = new User();
        u.name = "Prem";

        changeName(u);

        System.out.println(u.name);

    }

    static void changeName(User user) {
        user.name = "Rahul";
    }

}
```

Output

```
Rahul
```

Why?

```text
Stack
-----
u  ----->  Heap Object

method parameter gets copy of reference
user -----> same Heap Object
```

Both references point to **same object in heap**.

---

## 3. Reassigning Object Reference

If we reassign the reference, original object does **not change**.

```java
class User {
    String name;
}

public class Demo {

    public static void main(String[] args) {

        User u = new User();
        u.name = "Prem";

        change(u);

        System.out.println(u.name);

    }

    static void change(User user) {

        user = new User();
        user.name = "Rahul";

    }

}
```

Output

```
Prem
```

Because:

```text
user = new User()

now user points to new object
original object unchanged
```

---

## Summary

| Type | What is passed |
|-----|-----|
| Primitive | Copy of value |
| Object | Copy of reference |

Important rule:

```text
Java is strictly Pass-by-Value
```

Even for objects → **reference is copied, not the object**.

---

## One-line Interview Answer

**Java is strictly pass-by-value, meaning methods receive a copy of the variable. For objects, the copy of the reference is passed, allowing modification of the object's state but not the reference itself.**

## Observer Pattern (Java)

The **Observer Pattern** is a **behavioral design pattern** where **one object (Subject) notifies multiple dependent objects (Observers) when its state changes**.

It is also called the **publish–subscribe pattern**.

---

## Basic Idea

```text id="urct2v"
Subject (Publisher)
       ↓
Notifies
       ↓
Observers (Subscribers)
```

When the **subject changes**, all observers are **automatically updated**.

---

## Example Scenario

Example: **YouTube Channel**

```text id="3c2n1s"
Channel uploads video
        ↓
Subscribers get notification
```

---

## Step 1 — Observer Interface

```java id="u5t48s"
interface Observer {

    void update(String message);

}
```

---

## Step 2 — Subject Interface

```java id="u0ajy9"
interface Subject {

    void addObserver(Observer o);
    void removeObserver(Observer o);
    void notifyObservers();

}
```

---

## Step 3 — Concrete Subject

```java id="zftn41"
import java.util.*;

class YouTubeChannel implements Subject {

    private List<Observer> observers = new ArrayList<>();
    private String video;

    public void addObserver(Observer o){
        observers.add(o);
    }

    public void removeObserver(Observer o){
        observers.remove(o);
    }

    public void notifyObservers(){

        for(Observer o : observers){
            o.update(video);
        }

    }

    public void uploadVideo(String title){

        this.video = title;
        notifyObservers();

    }

}
```

---

## Step 4 — Observer Implementation

```java id="qacohq"
class Subscriber implements Observer {

    private String name;

    public Subscriber(String name){
        this.name = name;
    }

    public void update(String video){
        System.out.println(name + " notified: " + video);
    }

}
```

---

## Step 5 — Client Code

```java id="nlo20c"
public class Demo {

    public static void main(String[] args){

        YouTubeChannel channel = new YouTubeChannel();

        Observer s1 = new Subscriber("Prem");
        Observer s2 = new Subscriber("Rahul");

        channel.addObserver(s1);
        channel.addObserver(s2);

        channel.uploadVideo("Java Design Patterns");

    }

}
```

Output

```text id="z4jj9f"
Prem notified: Java Design Patterns
Rahul notified: Java Design Patterns
```

---

## Advantages

```text id="uc4zmy"
Loose coupling
Automatic updates
Scalable notification system
```

---

